<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Land of Punt</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Punt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Land of Punt was a region known to the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> as a source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, gold and exotic animals. Its exact location is uncertain but is generally placed along the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>. The Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> sent trading expeditions there, most famously under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/land-of-punt.jpg" alt="Land of Punt" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArdadN at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt_1450_BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hatshepsut, who ruled as pharaoh alongside the young Thutmose III from approximately 1479 to 1458 BCE, consolidated this inheritance through trade rather than war, dispatching the celebrated expedition to <b>Punt</b> and sponsoring an extraordinary building programme at Karnak and Deir el-Bahari.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors Mentuhotep III and Mentuhotep IV ruled from Thebes and sent expeditions to the quarries of the Wadi Hammamat and, by way of the Red Sea, towards <b>Punt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Old Kingdom shipbuilders used neither treenails nor metal fasteners: planks and superstructure were tightly tied and bound together with rope, and such ships sailed the length of the Red Sea to <b>Punt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
